INT. REGENCY LIBRARY - EVENING

Tall shelves, a crackling fire, a globe on a stand. Candles in sconces. Rain streaks the windowpanes.

LADY CORDELIA ASHWORTH-PEMBERTON, 30s, in an emerald gown, pours sherry. Her cousin, LADY HENRIETTA VANE, 30s, in a lilac dress, stands at the fireplace, fanning herself. LORD EDMUND FITZWILLIAM-OKAFOR, 40s, in a black coat, leafs through a book. Their butler, HOBSON, 60s, stands by the door with a salver.

CORDELIA
Henrietta, you are quiet tonight.

HENRIETTA
I am always quiet.

CORDELIA
You are never quiet.

HENRIETTA
Then I am tonight.

EDMUND
(without looking up)
She has a secret.

HENRIETTA
I do not.

EDMUND
Her fan has stopped. She only stops fanning when she is lying.

HENRIETTA
Cordelia, tell him he is insufferable.

CORDELIA
He is insufferable.

EDMUND
Thank you.

CORDELIA
It was not a compliment.

EDMUND
It was from you.

HENRIETTA
Enough. I will tell you.

CORDELIA
Do.

HENRIETTA
Father has accepted an offer for my hand.

CORDELIA
From whom?

HENRIETTA
From Lord Ravenscroft.

EDMUND
Ravenscroft is sixty.

HENRIETTA
Sixty-two.

CORDELIA
Henrietta.

HENRIETTA
He has a fine estate.

EDMUND
He has a fine gout.

CORDELIA
Do you wish to marry him?

HENRIETTA
I do not wish to do anything.

CORDELIA
Then do not.

HENRIETTA
It is not so simple.

EDMUND
It is exactly so simple. Tell your father no.

HENRIETTA
You do not know my father.

CORDELIA
I do. He is a dreadful man.

HENRIETTA
Cordelia!

CORDELIA
Well, he is.

Hobson steps forward and presents the salver. A single letter, sealed in red.

HOBSON
For Lady Henrietta, my lady.

Henrietta takes it with careful fingers. She opens it. Reads. Her fan drops. Cordelia and Edmund exchange a look.

HENRIETTA
(whispering)
He is dead.

EDMUND
Who?

HENRIETTA
Ravenscroft. This morning.

Silence. The fire pops. Cordelia sets down the sherry. She takes Henrietta's hand.

CORDELIA
Then you are free.

HENRIETTA
Yes. I am.

She laughs, a short, shocked sound. Then she cries. Edmund closes his book. Hobson quietly withdraws.
